x86_64-windows  a closure type has no name the standard obliges anybody to match - [expr.prim.lambda] calls it a unique unnamed type - and at namespace scope the two compilers spell it differently: clang for this ABI writes ??R<lambda_0>@@QEBA@H@Z and cxx1 writes ??R$_0@@QEBAHH@Z. Two differences, both measured here: the name itself, where cxx1 keeps the `$_N` it uses on the Itanium targets and clang-msvc writes `<lambda_N>`; and the return type, where clang encodes the call operator's deduced return as `@` and cxx1 writes the type it deduced, `H`. Nothing links against either - the call operator is reached through the object, and this object never leaves the default argument that made it. x86_64-linux and arm64-darwin agree with clang on all 5 names, this closure's included, which is what the case is here to check
